# Production build configuration. COMMITTED ON PURPOSE.
#
# Vite inlines VITE_* variables at build time, so this value ends up in the
# shipped bundle and is public by definition. It is a service address, not a
# secret; nothing here is sensitive.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN A DASHBOARD SETTING
# ----------------------------------------------------
# This was previously expected to come from a Vercel environment variable. It
# was never set, so the production bundle fell back to the empty default and
# the deployed frontend issued its API calls to http://127.0.0.1:8443 -- a
# developer laptop. Every login on the live site failed.
#
# Setting it in vercel.json's `build.env` did not work either: the deploy
# produced a byte-identical bundle (same index-*.js hash), still carrying the
# localhost fallback.
#
# A committed .env.production is read by Vite itself rather than by the hosting
# platform, so the build is reproducible from a clean clone on any host and the
# value is reviewable in the diff instead of living in dashboard state.
#
# Keep this in step with two other places, all three of which must name the
# same origin or login breaks:
#   - vercel.json          -> Content-Security-Policy connect-src
#   - render.yaml          -> NEXGEN_CORS_ORIGINS (the reverse direction)
VITE_IMATCH_API_BASE=https://nexgen-imatch-api.onrender.com
